description.narrative:
The Law on Contract-Based Vietnamese Overseas Workers (Law 69) was adopted by the National Assembly (NA) on 13 November 2020 and announced by the Vietnamese President's Office on 8 December 2020. 
The Law sets a better legal protection framework on labour migration, and the drafting process of the revised law was informed by tripartite and civil society dialogue, including with Vietnamese migrant workers. The law provides important general provisions that support the protection of migrant workers abroad and defines gender sensitive legal measures to protect rights of women migrant workers. 
Key changes include: 
•Removing the obligation for migrant workers to pay brokerage commissions and prohibition on recruitment agencies passing these costs on to workers;
•Removing the obligation for migrant workers to pay service fees to public employment services in line with Convention No. 88;
•If part or all of the service fee is covered by employers or overseas-worker receiving parties, then workers will only pay for any remaining amount required under Law;
•Ability for migrant workers to unilaterally liquidate contracts in situations of threats, sexual harassment, maltreatment or forced labour;
•Definitions for discrimination and forced labour in line with ILO Conventions No. 111 and Protocol P029;
•Provision for legal aid in cases of abuse, violence or discrimination;
•Specific wording about gender equality in the state goals;
•Prohibition on deceitful advertising for the purpose of organizing trafficking in persons, abusing recruitment activities to illegally collect fees, and charging brokerage fees 
•Pre-departure training must include information on: forced labour, trafficking in persons prevention, gender equality, sexual abuse, gender-based violence and prevention skills.
 With support of the Government of Australia, Canada, United States, and the European Union ILO contribution consisted of: 
ILO provided technical input on Law 69 and 5 sub laws to align with ILS and good practices. ILO worked with the Government to ensure that migrant workers, especially women, and their representative organizations participated in workshops. ILO conducted a legal gap analysis to ensure the law's alignment with CEDAW and ILS and supported the government's gender analysis report.  Some of ILO's key recommendations were incorporated.  Prior to National Assemble (NA) meetings, ILO submitted ILS, communications materials, and technical papers, which was an influential channel to access 483 NA Deputies, who subsequently discussed ILO's recommendations. 
ILO with Viet Nam Chamber of Commerce and Industry held a Private Sector-Government Dialogue to ensure that private sector and global brands present in Viet Nam and as employers/suppliers in countries of destination engaged in the legislative revision. 177 Government stakeholders, NA Deputies, private sector actors and anti-trafficking stakeholders participated.  The ILO Global Business Network on Forced Labour disseminated four policy briefs to mobilise Network members to advocate with Government on recruitment fees and costs to migrant workers during the Law 72 revision.
ILO, with the Academy of Journalism and Communications and Thomson Reuters Foundation delivered media training focusing on the negative impacts of recruitment fees and costs to migrant workers to increase media understanding of ILS to influence policy revision. Readership for articles published was 17,758.
ILO supported Vietnamese NA, Ministry of Labour, Invalids and Social Affairs, and Ministry of Foreign Affairs officials to take an ILO ITC Course on Labour Migration Governance and Coherence with Employment Policies to strengthen drafter's capacity to provide legislative revisions.
